      "rev_n_1_ac3e57_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_ac3e57_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_ac3e57",
        "thought_text": "The error in the previous solution was that it didn't correctly handle the case where segments might be rotated around the circle, potentially crossing from one end to the other. The current point of view is to check if the graph structure remains the same after rotation, which is not sufficient. Instead, we should consider the relative positions of segments and their endpoints after rotation. This approach will correctly identify rotational symmetry even when segments cross the circle's boundary.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_ac3e57_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_7cb62045c9",
        "created_at": 1794531560.7891634
      },
      "rev_n_2_69d5d5_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_69d5d5_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_69d5d5",
        "thought_text": "The error in the original code was in the divisibility check. The correct condition should be `n % cycle_length == 0`, not `n % cycle_length == 1`. This oversight caused the function to incorrectly identify some cases as rotationally symmetrical when they were not. The revised approach should focus on finding a cycle in the graph that divides the circle into equal parts, ensuring that the number of points on each segment is divisible by the cycle length.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_69d5d5_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_87407598d5",
        "created_at": 1794531594.6454527
      },
      "rev_n_3_bf47e7_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_bf47e7_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_bf47e7",
        "thought_text": "I need to reconsider the approach to checking for rotational symmetry. The current method of finding a cycle using DFS is insufficient, as it may not capture all possible rotations or handle disconnected components correctly. Instead, I should focus on analyzing the relative positions of segments rather than trying to find a specific cycle. This new approach would involve comparing the angles between segments and checking if they remain consistent under rotation.",
